    headers step 7, the instrument reads the tree as the compiler sees it (ARCH-HEADER-SPLIT 2; CEO-2032; the cto, the night of 2026-10-31): util_header_fanin.py takes its dependency lists from `make -B -n build-all` (the 297 compile lines with their flags, half a second) and `gcc -MM <flags> <unit>` per unit (eight at a time, ~10 s for the tree), never from the .d files. WHY: the build cache (util_build_cache.sh FORMAT 2) carries the producer root's .d files into every root that fetches, and a .d is rewritten only by a local compile of its unit, so on a cache-using root it is as old as that root's last local compile: 45 of this root's 308 named ir/IR.h, ir/SM.h, ir/pin_va.h, headers deleted weeks ago, and the ceiling gate, BLOCKING since SCRIP 208eab2b7 / harness 40a06d4, read 33 fan-in raises on the root while the worktree it was proven on (pristine .d) read 0; the step-7 row's `done` refused on it. The .d reader stays behind --from-d (and --obj, which implies it) for the gate's fixtures and for the equivalence proof: on the worktree, whose .d files are a pristine build's, the two readers print the same table, the same --ceiling, --unused, --bare and --ratchet all (0 differing lines) once the assembler leaves go through -MM with their -x assembler-with-cpp (their includes are real: a .d reader that skipped them lost 16 reachers of rt_diag.h). ARM 10 of test_gate_no_header_reaches_more_units_than_its_users.sh: a fixture with a Makefile, two units, and a stale .d under out/ naming a dead header reads fan-in 2 for the real header and nothing for the dead one, while --from-d reads the plant; red against the instrument before this change. The gate reads 0 on every arm on the root (cache-fetched) and on the worktree (pristine), the baseline unchanged.

